<ArticleLayout
  title="How to Extract Audio from a Video File (3 Methods)"
  description="Learn how to pull the MP3 or AAC audio track out of an MP4 video file using online tools, VLC, or dedicated software."
  canonicalUrl="/learn/guides/extract-audio-from-video/"
  category="Guides"
  categoryHref="/learn/guides/"
  datePublished="2024-01-21T08:00:00Z"
  readTime="4 min read"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  tocItems={tocItems}
  relatedArticles={relatedArticles}
>
  <p>Sometimes you download a music video, an interview, or a podcast, but you only want to listen to the audio track on your phone. Here are three ways to extract the audio from an existing video file.</p>

  <h2 id="online-downloader">Method 1: Use an Online Downloader (Easiest)</h2>
  <p>If you haven't downloaded the video yet, you can skip the extraction step entirely. Simply use a video downloader like mp4yt, paste the URL, and select the <strong>"Audio Only"</strong> or <strong>"MP3"</strong> option from the download menu. The server will extract the audio track directly from the platform's CDN, saving you time and bandwidth.</p>

  <h2 id="vlc-method">Method 2: Use VLC Media Player (Offline)</h2>
  <p>If you already have the MP4 file on your hard drive, you can use VLC to extract the audio.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open VLC and go to <strong>Media > Convert / Save</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Add</strong> and select your video file.</li>
    <li>Click the <strong>Convert / Save</strong> button at the bottom.</li>
    <li>In the "Profile" dropdown, select <strong>Audio - MP3</strong> or <strong>Audio - AAC</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose a destination file name (make sure it ends in `.mp3`), and click <strong>Start</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="dedicated-software">Method 3: Use Dedicated Software (Highest Quality)</h2>
  <p>If you want to extract the audio track losslessly (without re-encoding it and losing quality), you can use a free tool like <strong>Shutter Encoder</strong> or <strong>FFmpeg</strong>. These tools can strip the video track away and leave you with the exact, original audio file (usually AAC or Opus) in seconds.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
